[Event "Giuoco Piano (3...Bc5)"]

{White aims for slow play with c3 and d3, plus a timely d4. Black mirrors with ...d6 and ...a6/...Ba7. The fight is about the d4 square and the a2–g8 diagonal.}
1. e4 {[%csl Gd5,Gf5] Takes the centre and opens lines for the queen and the f1-bishop.}
1... e5 {Black claims an equal share of the centre.}
2. Nf3 {[%cal Gf3e5] Develops with an attack on e5.}
2... Nc6 {Defends e5 and develops.}
3. Bc4 {[%cal Gc4f7] The Italian: the bishop aims at f7, which only the king defends.}
3... Bc5 {[%cal Gc5f2] The Giuoco Piano. Black mirrors White and aims at f2.}
4. c3 {[%csl Gd4] Prepares d4 and opens a2 as a retreat for the bishop.}
4... Nf6 {[%cal Gf6e4] Counterattacks e4.}
(4... d6 {Solid. Black supports e5.}
 5. d4 {Takes the centre while Black hasn't hit e4.}
 5... exd4 {Black releases.}
 6. cxd4 {White gets the ideal pawn centre on d4 and e4.}
 6... Bb6 {The bishop keeps pressure on d4.}
 7. Nc3 {Development, supporting e4 and d5.}
 7... Nf6 {Attacks e4.}
 8. O-O {King safety. e4 is defended by the c3-knight.})
(4... Qe7 {Holds e5 strongly before ...d6.}
 5. d4 {Strikes in the centre.}
 5... Bb6 {Keeps the bishop on the a7–g1 diagonal.}
 6. O-O {King safety.}
 6... d6 {Solid.}
 7. a4 {[%cal Ga4a5] Threatens a5 to trap or harass the b6-bishop.}
 7... a6 {Makes room on a7.}
 8. h3 {[%csl Gg4] Takes g4 away from Black's pieces.})
5. d3 {[%csl Ge4] The modern Giuoco Pianissimo: White defends e4 and plays slowly.}
5... d6 {Solid. Black supports e5.}
(5... a6 {Prepares ...Ba7 to keep the bishop safe from d4 and b4.}
 6. O-O {King safety.}
 6... d6 {Solid.}
 7. a4 {Gains queenside space.}
 7... h6 {[%csl Gg5] Stops Bg5 pins.}
 8. Re1 {Supports e4 and prepares d4.}
 8... O-O {Castles.}
 9. h3 {[%csl Gg4] Stops ...Bg4 and ...Ng4.})
(5... O-O {Castles.}
 6. O-O {King safety.}
 6... d5 {[%cal Gd5e4] The active central break.}
 7. exd5 {Captures.}
 7... Nxd5 {Recaptures. Black's pieces are active, but e5 needs care.}
 8. a4 {Gains space and prepares Re1.}
 8... a6 {Makes room for the bishop on a7.}
 9. Re1 {[%cal Ge1e5] Pressure on e5.})
6. O-O {King safety.}
6... O-O {Castles.}
7. Re1 {[%cal Ge1e5] Supports e4 and prepares d4.}
7... a6 {Makes room on a7 for the c5-bishop.}
8. a4 {Gains queenside space and stops ...b5.}
8... Ba7 {The bishop is safe from d4 and b4.}
9. h3 {[%csl Gg4] Stops ...Bg4 pins and ...Ng4.}
9... h6 {[%csl Gg5] Stops Bg5.}
10. Nbd2 {[%cal Gd2f1] Heads for f1–g3, the classic Italian manoeuvre.}
10... Be6 {Challenges the c4-bishop.}
11. Bxe6 {Trades bishops.}
11... fxe6 {[%csl Gf8] Black recaptures toward the centre, and the f-file opens for the rook.} *

[Event "Two Knights (3...Nf6)"]

{Black counterattacks e4 at once. Against 4.d3, play is slow and positional.}
1. e4 {Takes the centre.}
1... e5 {Black claims the centre too.}
2. Nf3 {Attacks e5.}
2... Nc6 {Defends.}
3. Bc4 {[%cal Gc4f7] Aims at f7.}
3... Nf6 {[%cal Gf6e4] The Two Knights. It counterattacks e4.}
4. d3 {[%csl Ge4] The calm approach. It avoids the sharp 4.Ng5 lines.}
4... Be7 {Solid development.}
(4... h6 {[%csl Gg5] Stops Ng5 and Bg5.}
 5. O-O {King safety.}
 5... d6 {Solid.}
 6. c3 {Prepares d4.}
 6... g6 {Prepares ...Bg7.}
 7. Re1 {Prepares d4.}
 7... Bg7 {Fianchetto.}
 8. h3 {[%csl Gg4] Stops ...Ng4 and ...Bg4.})
(4... d5 {Black strikes at the centre.}
 5. exd5 {Captures.}
 5... Nxd5 {Recaptures.}
 6. O-O {King safety.}
 6... Be7 {Development.}
 7. Re1 {[%cal Ge1e5] Pressure on e5.}
 7... Bg4 {Pins.}
 8. h3 {Questions the bishop.}
 8... Bh5 {Keeps the pin.}
 9. Nbd2 {Development.})
5. O-O {King safety.}
5... O-O {Castles.}
6. Re1 {Supports e4.}
6... d6 {Solid.}
7. c3 {Prepares d4 and a retreat square on c2.}
7... Na5 {[%cal Ga5c4] Hits the bishop to trade it.}
8. Bb5 {Keeps the bishop.}
8... a6 {Hits the bishop.}
9. Ba4 {Retreats.}
9... b5 {Gains space with tempo.}
10. Bc2 {The bishop stays on the b1–h7 diagonal.}
10... c5 {Space on the queenside.}
11. Nbd2 {[%cal Gd2f1] Heads for f1–g3.} *
